  Forgive me if this is a dumb question, but I've just been
thinking about recovery procedures on a Tru64 system
and I'm not sure what the best approach might be.

  I back up everything with Networker, and for failures
which don't damage the system ( or the /nsr directory ) it
appears that restoring with Networker is pretty
straightforward. What about the cases where Networker
isn't available though ( because / or /usr has been
trashed)?  I presume that for those situations one
would fall back to a dump of those file systems, but
how does one "bootstrap" the recovery process?

 I'm basically a VMS person - in that world one would do
an image backup of the system disk and use standalone
backup to restore that image if the system disk failed.
I don't see the equivalent functionality in Tru64, if
it's there pointers would be appreciated.

  The approach I've come up ( untested as of yet ) would be:

   - keep relatively current dumps ( or vdumps ) of / , /usr
and /var file systems.

   - in event of catastrophic failure, do a full install
of Tru64 onto replacement system disk ( which will wipe all
exising data ). Boot that system and then vrestore the /,
/usr and /var file systems onto the new system ( is it
safe to restore / like this while running in single-user
mode? ).

  - Reboot system after restoring these dumps. Networker
should now be available ( assuming it was installed before
the dumps were created ). Use Networker to restore the
current versions of files in those file systems from the
current Networker savesets.

   This seems like a rather awkward approach, so I'm hoping
someone can point me to a better one. The goal is to have
a backup procedure which can be pretty much automated
(not require frequent periods of single user mode ) but
still produce a backup which can recover from catastrophic
failures.

