And in a directory on the first filesystem to be backed up (usually /)
and on a floppy in the otherwise useless but always present floppy drives
on an alpha.
You may as well tar up all of /etc since some of the other bits in there
(sysconfigtab rc.config password file) are all fairly useful to have
around.
Oh and if you have any exotic bits then a copy of your kernel configuration
file is always a good thing to have on hand as well.
Another one which caught us a while ago, make sure that all of your console
environment variables are recorded somewhere, the way that the replacement
machine is setup may well not match your configuration even though the
hardware matches.
Last and most important of all, test it regularly and rigorously, get in
replacement kit, build a new system from scratch and make sure that you
do it with only the things in your disaster  recovery toolkit.
If you can't then update the documentation and the toolkit to match what
you needed and do it again.
If at all possible, get someone who has no familiarity with the system to
do the recovery.
Adam Price







