Hi

I have always found it to be a good idea not to exceed about 90% of a disk

even when using fixed size database files. This is necessary when backing
up
using "vdump". Recovery using "vrestore" will create a tempory file that
is
likely to fill up disk.

A possible way to fool the recovery is to suspend "vrestore" and move the
"restore" tempory file to a different disk and create a soft link. If you
are 100%
full though you still may have a problem.

Since you mentioned partitions, I assume you are using the Unix File
System.
Unless you really need to use UFS (minor performance improvement over
AdvFS), I would recomend you use AdvFS. By doing this you are not faced
with fixed length partitions and you can if required use "cloning" or even

grow a domain. You need the Advanced AdvFS Utilities for this.

AdvFS cloning is very good for databases such as Oracle on AdvFS since all

you need to do to backup a database is 1) Suspend the database. 2) Clone
the
appropriate filesets. 3) Start the database. 4) Backup the cloned data.
You
should not allow your domain to fill to much more than 90% though.

Using cloning and then backing up works well, however you will increase
I/O
which will impact the performance of the database during backup. Also a
word
of caution "Don't run defragmention software on filesets that are being
backed
up".

I hope this helps.

Regards Don

