I won't speak for engineering on this, but if the installation
scripts and program care more about the physical location of
a file system than the logical location, they're horribly
broken.  Most programs shouldn't care whether /usr is on the
same disk, a different disk or halfway across the county on
the other side of an ATM bridge (ok, this might cause excess
latency...).

If the location has the necessary read/write access, can be
mounted as part of the normal start-up process that mounts
file systems and can be opened by its pathname then the
location doesn't matter.
