Organization: Imperial College
Newsgroups: comp.unix.tru64

Before doing anything as drastic as newfs check that the "unused"
partition is really spare. Run disklabel rz17 and compare the partition
sizes in the label with the used disk partitions in the df output. The
cylinder sizes in the label show the area covered by each partition. 
From your listing if appears that either rz17h is included in another
partition, in which case you can't use it, or the entire rz17 disk is
not in use. If there are no other rz17 partitions in the df output that
is probably the case.
Only if the entire disk is unused the disklabel commands
	disklabel -rw rz17a junk	# install factory label
	disklabel -re rz17		# to edit the label
		can be used.
Use newfs to create the new file systems on the require partitions. If
you want to use the entire disk as one partition you can skip editing 
the label and use newfs /dev/rz17c. The will overwrite the whole disk
with a new empty file system. 
-- 

                             Jeff Wilson

