In article <Pine.LNX.4.10.10005031729440.1883-100000@queeg.uwaterloo.ca>, Mike Hudson <mjhudson@uwaterloo.ca> writes:

>I got a DS20E recently, running Tru64 V4.0E.  The factory installation
>had an a, b (swap) and a small g partition (about 700 MB) on the first of
>my four 18Gb disks.  g is where /usr in located.
>
>I would like to make this g partition bigger. Can I go ahead and resize it
>using the Disk Configuration tool?  Somewhere I remember reading that you
>can't modify partitions if they are in use, which I assume /usr is if I am
>running the Disk Config in the first place ... 

You can't change the size or offset of a partition that is open; has
a file system mounted on it, in use for page/swap space, etc.

Even if the partition were closed, you can't usefully change the size
because neither UFS nor AdvFS has any tools that will convince the
file system that the underlying space has grown.  You might be able
to find Open source for a program that can do this with UFS, but
you'd want to be very careful.

If you're using AdvFS and have the utilities license, you can expand
the space online using addvol.  You can either just add a device to
the domain or migrate the files to a new device by adding the new
one and removing the old one.  Doing this twice will give the
appearance of what you want when done:

1.  Add device to existing /usr domain.
2.  Remove existing device and wait for files to be migrated.
3.  Resize partition of original device.
4.  Add it do /usr domain.
5.  Remove device from #1.

The general solution which works for any file system, is to
create a new file system of the desired size, backup the
existing data, restore it on the target.  You can do these
last two steps across a pipe:

# dump 0f - /usr | ( cd /mnt ; restore rf - )

For /usr it would be best to do this running single user.  I
have indeed left out the steps of creating and mounting
the file system for /mnt.  If you're using AdvFS, then
use vdump instead of dump, vrestore instead of restore 
and the 'x' option on vrestore instead of 'r' on restore.

>Don't want to risk destroying the data talready there, so advice is
>appreciated.

If you have a backup of your system, the risk of losing something
is pretty low.  If you don't have a backup, why not?

>
>Mike

